Looking at this file, I can see several functions: `and_n_others`, `get_updated_changelog`, `display_list`, `render_markdown_path`, and `webpack_entry`.

The `display_list` function stands out — it formats lists with proper English conjunctions and truncation. The docstring says "summarizing when you have more than `display_limit`" and shows that with a limit of 3, four items produce "Jessica, Waseem, Tim, and 1 other". But looking at the condition `len(values) <= display_limit`, with display_limit=3 and 4 values, 4 <= 3 is False so it enters the else branch and truncates. However, the comment says "Fewer than `display_limit` values" while `<=` actually means "fewer than or equal to", suggesting the boundary is off by one — users would expect display_limit=3 to mean "at most 3 items shown" but the behavior allows 4 items before truncation triggers.

